592186558501 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-4 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-4$ and ${y}_{2}=-5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-4 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=-\\frac{5}{2}$ and ${x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{1}=(-\\frac{3}{2})\\cdot (-4)=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558528 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558528 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107500" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558528 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558528 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558528 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "758b84b6-7328-584d-83ee-1df88702770d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558528 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=4 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=4$ and ${y}_{2}=5.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}8{x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=4 \\\\ {x}_{2}=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=5$ and ${x}_{1}=3.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}+{y}_{2}=3+5=8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558529 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558415 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558529 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107185" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558529 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558529 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the matrix $L$ and the vector $b,$ given by \n\n$$\nL=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $A=LU$ is the LU factorization of some $3\\times 3$ matrix $A,$ find the vector $y$ such that\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nis equivalent to $Ax=b.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558529 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "772e6d55-2cc0-5849-b50b-b1451b60fe6e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558529 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 8 & -5 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -2{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 8{y}_{1}-5{y}_{2}+{y}_{3}=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can compute the solution to this system using forward substitution, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & =-1, \\\\ {y}_{2} & =-3+2{y}_{1} \\\\  & =-3+2(-1) \\\\  & =-5, \\\\ {y}_{3} & =8-8{y}_{1}+5{y}_{2} \\\\  & =8-8(-1)+5(-5) \\\\  & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n$y=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -9\\end{aligned}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558571 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186558433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558571 #edb/ref 1069 "q-107491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558571 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558571 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the LU decomposition of the matrix $A$ and the vector $b,$ given by\n\n$$\nA={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U},\\,b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $⟨{x}_{1},{x}_{2}⟩$ and $⟨{y}_{1},{y}_{2}⟩$ are the solutions to the systems $Ax=b$ and $Ly=b$ respectively, what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558571 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e26325a0-a17c-5d3b-96e5-ffcb13cdecc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186558571 #edb/ref 1074 "First, notice that since $A=LU,$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax=b\\,⟹\\,{{(LU)}_{⏟}}_{A}x=b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, setting $Ux=y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\nL{{(Ux)}_{⏟}}_{y}=b\\,⟹\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the two systems in turn.\n\n- First, we solve $Ly=b.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=b=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-3 \\\\ 3{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${y}_{1}=-3$ and ${y}_{2}=2.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\ny=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n- Next, we solve $Ux=y.$ Writing this equation in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=y=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4{x}_{1}+{x}_{2}=-3 \\\\ 2{x}_{2}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is ${x}_{2}=1$ and ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nx=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, ${x}_{1}\\cdot {y}_{2}=(-1)\\cdot 2=-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
